<hr />
<h1>Automata Theory Introduction</h1>
<h2>Automata &minus; What is it?</h2>
<p>The term "Automata" is derived from the Greek word "Œ±·ΩêœÑœåŒºŒ±œÑŒ±" which means "self-acting". An automaton (Automata in plural) is an abstract self-propelled computing device which follows a predetermined sequence of operations automatically.</p>
<p>An automaton with a finite number of states is called a <b>Finite Automaton</b> (FA) or <b>Finite State Machine</b> (FSM).</p>
<h3>Formal definition of a Finite Automaton</h3>
<p>An automaton can be represented by a 5-tuple (Q, &sum;, Œ¥, q<sub>0</sub>, F), where &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Q</b> is a finite set of states.</p></li>
<li><p><b>&sum;</b> is a finite set of symbols, called the <b>alphabet</b> of the automaton.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Œ¥</b> is the transition function.</p></li>
<li><p><b>q<sub>0</sub></b> is the initial state from where any input is processed (q<sub>0</sub> &isin; Q).</p></li>
<li><p><b>F</b> is a set of final state/states of Q (F &sube; Q).</p></li>
</ul>
<h2>Related Terminologies</h2>
<h3>Alphabet</h3>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Definition</b> &minus; An <b>alphabet</b> is any finite set of symbols.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Example</b> &minus; &Sum; = {a, b, c, d} is an <b>alphabet set</b> where ‚Äòa‚Äô, ‚Äòb‚Äô, ‚Äòc‚Äô, and ‚Äòd‚Äô are <b>alphabets</b>.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3>String</h3>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Definition</b> &minus; A <b>string</b> is a finite sequence of symbols taken from &sum;.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Example</b> &minus; ‚Äòcabcad‚Äô is a valid string on the alphabet set &sum; = {a, b, c,d}</p></li>
</ul>
<h3>Length of a String</h3>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Definition</b> &minus; It is the number of symbols present in a string. (Denoted by <b>|S|</b>).</p></li>
<li><p><b>Examples</b> &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li><p>If S=‚Äòcabcad‚Äô, |S|= 6</p></li>
<li><p>If |S|= 0, it is called an <b>empty string</b> (Denoted by <b>&lambda;</b> or <b>&epsilon;</b>)</p></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Kleene Star</h3>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Definition</b> &minus; The set <b>&sum;<sup>*</sup></b> is the infinite set of all possible strings of all possible lengths over <b>&sum;</b> including <b>&lambda;</b>.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Representation</b> &minus; &sum;<sup>*</sup> = &sum;<sub>0</sub> &cup; &sum;<sub>1</sub> &cup; &sum;<sub>2</sub> &cup;‚Ä¶‚Ä¶.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Example</b> &minus; If &sum; = {a, b}, &sum;<sup>*</sup>= {&lambda;, a, b, aa, ab, ba, bb,‚Ä¶‚Ä¶‚Ä¶..}</p></li>
</ul>
<h3>Kleene Closure/Plus</h3>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Definition</b> &minus; The set <b>&sum;<sup>&plus;</sup></b> is the infinite set of all possible strings of all possible lengths over <b>&sum;</b> excluding <b>&lambda;</b>.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Representation</b> &minus; &sum;<sup>&plus;</sup> = &sum;<sub>0</sub> &cup; &sum;<sub>1</sub> &cup; &sum;<sub>2</sub> &cup;‚Ä¶‚Ä¶.</p>
<p style="padding-left:23%;">&sum;<sup>&plus;</sup> = &sum;<sup>*</sup> ‚àí { &lambda; }</p></li>
<li><p><b>Example</b> &minus; If &sum; = { a, b } , &sum;<sup>&plus;</sup> ={ a, b, aa, ab, ba, bb,‚Ä¶‚Ä¶‚Ä¶..}</p></li>
</ul>
<h3>Language</h3>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Definition</b> &minus; A language is a subset of &sum;<sup>*</sup> for some alphabet &sum;. It can be finite or infinite.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Example</b> &minus; If the language takes all possible strings of length 2 over &sum; = {a, b}, then L = { ab, bb, ba, bb}</p></li>
</ul>
<h1>Deterministic Finite Automaton</h1>
<p>Finite Automaton can be classified into two types &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li>Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA)</li>
<li>Non-deterministic Finite Automaton (NDFA / NFA)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA)</h3>
<p>In DFA, for each input symbol, one can determine the state to which the machine will move. Hence, it is called <b>Deterministic Automaton.</b> As it has a finite number of states, the machine is called <b>Deterministic Finite Machine</b> or <b>Deterministic Finite Automaton.</b></p>
<p><b>Formal Definition of a DFA</b></p>
<p>A DFA can be represented by a 5-tuple (Q, &sum;, Œ¥, q<sub>0</sub>, F) where &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Q</b> is a finite set of states.</p></li>
<li><p><b>&sum;</b> is a finite set of symbols called the alphabet.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Œ¥</b> is the transition function where Œ¥: Q &times; &sum; &rarr; Q</p></li>
<li><p><b>q<sub>0</sub></b> is the initial state from where any input is processed (q0 &isin; Q).</p></li>
<li><p><b>F</b> is a set of final state/states of Q (F &sube; Q).</p></li>
</ul>
<p><b>Graphical Representation of a DFA</b></p>
<p>A DFA is represented by digraphs called <b>state diagram.</b></p>
<ul class="list">
<li>The vertices represent the states.</li>
<li>The arcs labeled with an input alphabet show the transitions.</li>
<li>The initial state is denoted by an empty single incoming arc.</li>
<li>The final state is indicated by double circles.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>Let a deterministic finite automaton be &rarr;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li>Q = {a, b, c},</li>
<li>&sum; = {0, 1},</li>
<li>q<sub>0</sub>={a},</li>
<li>F={c}, and</li>
<li>Transition function Œ¥ as shown by the following table &minus;</li>
</ul>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th>Present State</th>
<th>Next State for Input 0</th>
<th>Next State for Input 1</th>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td><b>A</b></td>
<td>a</td>
<td>b</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td><b>B</b></td>
<td>c</td>
<td>a</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td><b>C</b></td>
<td>b</td>
<td>c</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p>Its graphical representation would be as follows &minus;</p>
<img src="/automata_theory/images/dfa_graphical_representation.jpg" alt="DFA Graphical Representation" />
<h1>Non-deterministic Finite Automaton</h1>
<p>In NDFA, for a particular input symbol, the machine can move to any combination of the states in the machine. In other words, the exact state to which the machine moves cannot be determined. Hence, it is called <b>Non-deterministic Automaton.</b> As it has finite number of states, the machine is called <b>Non-deterministic Finite Machine</b> or <b>Non-deterministic Finite Automaton</b>.</p>
<p><b>Formal Definition of an NDFA</b></p>
<p>An NDFA can be represented by a 5-tuple (Q, &sum;, Œ¥, q<sub>0</sub>, F) where &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Q</b> is a finite set of states.</p></li>
<li><p><b>&sum;</b> is a finite set of symbols called the alphabets.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Œ¥</b> is the transition function where Œ¥: Q &times; {&sum; &cup; &epsilon;} &rarr; 2Q (Here the power set of Q (2<sup>Q</sup>) has been taken because in case of NDFA, from a state, transition can occur to any combination of Q states)</p></li>
<li><p><b>q<sub>0</sub></b> is the initial state from where any input is processed (q<sub>0</sub> &isin; Q).</p></li>
<li><p><b>F</b> is a set of final state/states of Q (F &sube; Q).</p></li>
</ul>
<p><b>Graphical Representation of an NDFA &minus; (same as DFA)</b></p>
<p>An NDFA is represented by digraphs called state diagram.</p>
<ul class="list">
<li>The vertices represent the states.</li>
<li>The arcs labeled with an input alphabet show the transitions.</li>
<li>The initial state is denoted by an empty single incoming arc.</li>
<li>The final state is indicated by double circles.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>Let a non-deterministic finite automaton be &rarr;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li>Q = {a, b, c}</li>
<li>&sum; = {0, 1}</li>
<li>q<sub>0</sub> = {a}</li>
<li>F={c}</li>
<li>The transition function Œ¥ as shown below &minus;</li>
</ul>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th>Present State</th>
<th>Next State for Input 0</th>
<th>Next State for Input 1</th>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>a</td>
<td>a, b</td>
<td>b</td>
</tr>
<tr  >
<td>b</td>
<td>c</td>
<td>a, c</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>c</td>
<td>b, c</td>
<td>c</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p>Its graphical representation would be as follows &minus;</p>
<img src="/automata_theory/images/ndfa_graphical_representation.jpg" alt="NDFA Graphical Representation" />
<h2>DFA vs NDFA</h2>
<p>The following table lists the differences between DFA and NDFA.</p>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th style="text-align:center;">DFA</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">NDFA</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>The transition from a state is to a single particular next state for each input symbol. Hence it is called <i>deterministic</i>.</td>
<td>The transition from a state can be to multiple next states for each input symbol. Hence it is called <i>non-deterministic</i>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Empty string transitions are not seen in DFA.</td>
<td>NDFA permits empty string transitions.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Backtracking is allowed in DFA</td>
<td>In NDFA, backtracking is not always possible.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Requires more space.</td>
<td>Requires less space.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>A string is accepted by a DFA, if it transits to a final state.</td>
<td>A string is accepted by a NDFA, if at least one of all possible transitions ends in a final state.</td>
</tr>
</table>
<h2>Acceptors, Classifiers, and Transducers</h2>
<h3>Acceptor (Recognizer)</h3>
<p>An automaton that computes a Boolean function is called an <b>acceptor.</b> All the states of an acceptor is either accepting or rejecting the inputs given to it.</p>
<h3>Classifier</h3>
<p>A <b>classifier</b> has more than two final states and it gives a single output when it terminates.</p>
<h3>Transducer</h3>
<p>An automaton that produces outputs based on current input and/or previous state is called a <b>transducer.</b> Transducers can be of two types &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li><p><b>Mealy Machine</b> &minus; The output depends both on the current state and the current input.</p></li>
<li><p><b>Moore Machine</b> &minus; The output depends only on the current state.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2>Acceptability by DFA and NDFA</h2>
<p>A string is accepted by a DFA/NDFA iff the DFA/NDFA starting at the initial state ends in an accepting state (any of the final states) after reading the string wholly.</p>
<p>A string S is accepted by a DFA/NDFA (Q, &sum;, Œ¥, q<sub>0</sub>, F), iff</p>
<p style="padding-left:15%;"><b>Œ¥<sup>*</sup>(q<sub>0</sub>, S) &isin; F</b></p>
<p>The language <b>L</b> accepted by DFA/NDFA is</p>
<p style="padding-left:15%;"><b>{S | S &isin; &sum;<sup>*</sup> and Œ¥<sup>*</sup>(q<sub>0</sub>, S) &isin; F}</b></p>
<p>A string S‚Ä≤ is not accepted by a DFA/NDFA (Q, &sum;, Œ¥, q<sub>0</sub>, F), iff</p>
<p style="padding-left:15%;"><b>Œ¥<sup>*</sup>(q<sub>0</sub>, S‚Ä≤) &notin; F</b></p>
<p>The language L‚Ä≤ not accepted by DFA/NDFA (Complement of accepted language L) is</p>
<p style="padding-left:15%;"><b>{S | S &isin; &sum;<sup>*</sup> and Œ¥<sup></sup>*(q<sub>0</sub>, S) &notin; F}</b></p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Let us consider the DFA shown in Figure 1.3. From the DFA, the acceptable strings can be derived.</p>
<img src="/automata_theory/images/acceptability_of_strings_by_dfa.jpg" alt="Acceptability of String by DFA" />
<p>Strings accepted by the above DFA &minus; {0, 00, 11, 010, 101, ...........}</p>
<p>Strings not accepted by the above DFA &minus; {1, 011, 111, ........}</p>
<h1>NDFA to DFA Conversion</h1>
<h3>Problem Statement</h3>
<p>Let <b>X = (Q<sub>x</sub>, &sum;, Œ¥<sub>x</sub>, q<sub>0</sub>, F<sub>x</sub>)</b> be an NDFA which accepts the language L(X). We have to design an equivalent DFA <b>Y = (Q<sub>y</sub>, &sum;, Œ¥<sub>y</sub>, q<sub>0</sub>, F<sub>y</sub>)</b> such that <b>L(Y) = L(X).</b> The following procedure converts the NDFA to its equivalent DFA &minus;</p>
<h3>Algorithm</h3>
<table>
<tr>
<td><b>Input:</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">An NDFA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Output:</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">equivalent DFA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Step 1</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Create state table from the given NDFA.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Step 2</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Create a blank state table under possible input alphabets for the equivalent DFA.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Step 3</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Mark the start state of the DFA by q<sub>0</sub> (Same as the NDFA).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 4</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Find out the combination of States {Q<sub>0</sub>, Q<sub>1</sub>,... , Q<sub>n</sub>} for each possible input alphabet.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 5</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Each time we generate a new DFA state under the input alphabet columns, we have to apply step 4 again, otherwise go to step 6.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 6</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">The states which contain any of the final states of the NDFA are the final states of the equivalent DFA.</td>
</tr>
</table>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>The NDFA table is as follows &minus;</p>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th style="text-align:center;">q</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">Œ¥(q,0)</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">Œ¥(q,1)</th>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>a</td>
<td>{a,b,c,d,e}</td>
<td>{d,e}</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>b</td>
<td>{c}</td>
<td>{e}</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>c</td>
<td>&empty;</td>
<td>{b}</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>d</td>
<td>{e}</td>
<td>&empty;</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>e</td>
<td>&empty;</td>
<td>&empty;</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p>Let us consider the NDFA shown in the figure below.</p>
<img src="/automata_theory/images/ndfa.jpg" alt="NDFA" />
<p>Using above algorithm, we find its equivalent DFA. The state table of the DFA is shown in below.</p>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th style="text-align:center;">q</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">Œ¥(q,0)</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">Œ¥(q,1)</th>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>a</td>
<td>{a,b,c,d,e}</td>
<td>{d,e}</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>{a,b,c,d,e}</td>
<td>{a,b,c,d,e}</td>
<td>{b,d,e}</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>{d,e}</td>
<td>e</td>
<td>&empty;</td>
</tr>
