<h1>DFA Minimization</h1>
<h2>DFA Minimization using Myphill-Nerode Theorem</h2>
<h3>Algorithm</h3>
<table>
<tr>
<td><b>Input:</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">DFA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><b>Output:</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Minimized DFA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 1</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Draw a table for all pairs of states (Q<sub>i</sub>, Q<sub>j</sub>) not necessarily connected directly [All are unmarked initially]</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 2</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Consider every state pair (Q<sub>i</sub>, Q<sub>j</sub>) in the DFA where Q<sub>i</sub> &isin; F and Q<sub>j</sub> &notin; F or vice versa and mark them. [Here F is the set of final states].</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 3</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;"><p>Repeat this step until we cannot mark anymore states &minus;</p>
<p>If there is an unmarked pair (Q<sub>i</sub>, Q<sub>j</sub>), mark it if the pair {Œ¥(Q<sub>i</sub>, A), Œ¥ (Q<sub>i</sub>, A)} is marked for some input alphabet.</p></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;"><b>Step 4</b></td>
<td style="padding-left:3%;">Combine all the unmarked pair (Q<sub>i</sub>, Q<sub>j</sub>) and make them a single state in the reduced DFA.</td>
</tr>
</table>
<h3>Example</h3>
