<img src="/automata_theory/images/state_diagram_of_reduced_dfa.jpg" alt="State Diagram of Reduced DFA" />
<h2>DFA Minimization using Equivalence Theorem</h2>
<p>If X and Y are two states in a DFA, we can combine these two states into {X, Y} if they are not distinguishable. Two states are distinguishable, if there is at least one string S, such that one of Œ¥ (X, S) and Œ¥ (Y, S) is accepting and another is not accepting. Hence, a DFA is minimal if and only if all the states are distinguishable.</p>
<h3>Algorithm</h3>
<table>
<tr>
<td style="width:10%;vertical-align:top;font-weight:bold;">Step 1</td>
<td >All the states <b>Q</b> are divided in two partitions &minus; <b>final states</b> and <b>non-final states</b> and are denoted by <b>P<sub>0</sub></b>. All the states in a partition are 0<sup>th</sup> equivalent. Take a counter <b>k</b> and initialize it with 0.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="vertical-align:top;font-weight:bold;">Step 2</td>
<td>Increment k by 1. For each partition in P<sub>k</sub>, divide the states in P<sub>k</sub> into two partitions if they are k-distinguishable. Two states within this partition X and Y are k-distinguishable if there is an input <b>S</b> such that <b>Œ¥(X, S)</b> and <b>Œ¥(Y, S)</b> are (k-1)-distinguishable.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="font-weight:bold;">Step 3</td>
<td>If P<sub>k</sub> ‚â† P<sub>k-1</sub>, repeat Step 2, otherwise go to Step 4.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="font-weight:bold;">Step 4</td>
<td>Combine k<sup>th</sup> equivalent sets and make them the new states of the reduced DFA.</td>
</tr>
</table>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>Let us consider the following DFA &minus;</p>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th style="text-align:center;">q</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">Œ¥(q,0)</th>
<th style="text-align:center;">Œ¥(q,1)</th>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>a</td>
<td>b</td>
<td>c</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>b</td>
<td>a</td>
<td>d</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>c</td>
<td>e</td>
<td>f</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>d</td>
<td>e</td>
<td>f</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>e</td>
<td>e</td>
<td>f</td>
</tr>
<tr style="text-align:center;">
<td>f</td>
<td>f</td>
<td>f</td>
</tr>
</table>
<img src="/automata_theory/images/dfa.jpg" alt="DFA" />
<p>Let us apply above algorithm to the above DFA &minus;</p>
<ul class="list">
<li>P<sub>0</sub> = {(c,d,e), (a,b,f)}</li>
<li>P<sub>1</sub> = {(c,d,e), (a,b),(f)}</li>
<li>P<sub>2</sub> = {(c,d,e), (a,b),(f)}</li>
</ul>
<p>Hence, P<sub>1</sub> = P<sub>2</sub>.</p>
<p>There are three states in the reduced DFA. The reduced DFA is as follows &minus;</p>
<p>The State table of DFA is as follows &minus;</p>
<table class="table table-bordered">
<tr>
<th style="text-align:center;">Q</th>
